{"key":"apcalcab-mcq-024","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"D","final_answer":"2.5","working":"To find lim(x→∞) (5x²−x+4)/(2x²+3), divide the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of x in the denominator, which is x²: (5 - 1/x + 4/x²) / (2 + 3/x²). As x approaches infinity, the terms 1/x, 4/x², and 3/x² all approach 0. Therefore, the limit is (5 - 0 + 0) / (2 + 0) = 5/2 = 2.5.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":372,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":372,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":355,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":163,"reasoningTokens":192},"totalTokens":727,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":192,"promptTokenCount":372,"candidatesTokenCount":163,"totalTokenCount":727,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":372}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":163}]},"reasoningTokens":192,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":4398,"keyed_label":"D"}
